  "result": "The command now prints 27.06 instead of 26.09, and all 6 tests pass.\n\n**Root cause:** in `to_cents` (`shop/money.py:9`), a price with one decimal place had its zero added on the wrong side. `\"12.5\"` became `\"12.05\"`, so it was read as 1205 cents instead of 1250. Two of those plus 8.25% tax gave 26.09.\n\n**Fix:** I changed `frac.rjust(2, \"0\")` to `frac.ljust(2, \"0\")` so the zero goes after the digit. I also checked a few other prices by hand: `12.5` \u2192 1250, `12` \u2192 1200, `12.05` \u2192 1205, `0.5` \u2192 50.\n\n**Tests:** pytest isn't installed here, so I ran them with `python3 -m unittest`. No existing test covers one-decimal prices, which is why the bug got through. I didn't add one; a small test for `to_cents(\"12.5\") == 1250` would catch this if it comes back.",
